 Cartique v1 — Design Philosophy

 Introduction

Cartique was not designed to be another e-commerce platform.

It was designed as a Transactional Commerce Kernel—a small, deterministic core capable of expressing almost any commercial transaction through a consistent set of primitives.

The objective is not to build software for selling products. The objective is to build software that understands commercial intent, evaluates that intent deterministically, and produces immutable commercial truth.

Everything else is an implementation detail.



 The Fundamental Shift

Traditional commerce platforms model features.

Cartique models concepts.

Instead of introducing dedicated systems for subscriptions, configurable products, bundles, rentals, bookings, financing, or wholesale pricing, Cartique asks a different question:

> What are the smallest concepts that every commercial transaction has in common?

Those concepts became the foundation of the system.

```
Catalog Item

↓

Offer Template

↓

Configuration

↓

Offer Resolution

↓

Order Snapshot
```

Every commerce experience is simply a different composition of these primitives.



 Commerce as Compilation

One of the core ideas behind Cartique is that commerce is not a collection of mutable database records.

Commerce is a compilation process.

The customer expresses intent.

```
"I want this."

↓

Configuration
```

The platform evaluates that intent against business rules and commercial context.

```
Configuration
        +
Commercial Context

↓

Resolution Engine
```

The result is not a modified configuration.

The result is a new object describing commercial truth.

```
Offer Resolution
```

Nothing inside the original intent changes.

Intent remains immutable.

Truth is computed.



 Intent Before Context

A customer's configuration represents only what they want.

It deliberately contains no pricing, taxation, inventory, customer information, shipping calculations, or operational state.

A configuration should be portable.

It should be possible to:

 save it
 share it
 bookmark it
 email it
 cache it
 restore it years later

without carrying any commercial assumptions.

Context is introduced only during resolution.

```
Configuration
        +
Context

↓

Offer Resolution
```

Intent is timeless.

Commercial truth is contextual.



 Resolution Is Pure

The Resolution Engine is not an operational workflow.

It performs no mutations.

It allocates nothing.

It reserves nothing.

It charges nothing.

It simply answers the question:

> "Given this configuration and this context, what is commercially true?"

For identical inputs, the output must always be identical.

```
(Configuration, Context)

↓

Offer Resolution
```

No hidden state.

No side effects.

No randomness.

No environmental dependencies.

Pure computation.



 Business Rules Are Not Business Objects

Traditional systems embed pricing, discounts, tax, inventory, and shipping logic directly into products or orders.

Cartique deliberately separates them.

Business rules belong to specialized providers.

```
Configuration

↓

Pricing Provider

↓

Pricing Delta
```

```
Configuration

↓

Inventory Provider

↓

Inventory Delta
```

Each provider solves one problem.

The compiler assembles the complete picture.



 Composition Over Specialization

Cartique does not have product types.

It has compositions.

A configurable laptop.

A rental vehicle.

A hotel booking.

A financing agreement.

A subscription.

All emerge from combining the same primitives.

Complexity is achieved through composition rather than inheritance.



 Templates Define Possibility

Offer Templates describe what may be configured.

They define:

 selections
 constraints
 supported capabilities

A template is a contract between the business and the user interface.

The frontend does not invent configuration screens.

It renders the template.

```
Offer Template

↓

Selection Model

↓

Generated UI
```

The same template can power:

 Web
 Mobile
 POS
 API
 CLI
 AI assistants

without modification.



 Separation of Knowledge

Every object knows only what it must know.

Catalog Items know how products are described.

Offer Templates know how products may be configured.

Selection Models know what choices are valid.

Configurations know customer intent.

Resolution Providers know how to calculate commercial truth.

Workflows know how to execute operations.

Order Snapshots know historical truth.

No layer reaches across its boundary.

Knowledge is intentionally limited.



 Replaceable Runtime

The runtime exists to execute the domain.

It is not the domain itself.

The execution engine may evolve.

Today's runtime may execute locally.

Tomorrow's runtime may execute on distributed workers.

Next year's runtime may execute inside a serverless workflow engine.

The domain remains unchanged.

```
Domain

↓

Compiler

↓

Runtime
```

The runtime is replaceable.

The domain is sacred.



 Commercial Truth Is Temporary

An Offer Resolution represents the commercial truth of a configuration at a specific moment.

Inventory changes.

Tax laws change.

Discounts expire.

Exchange rates fluctuate.

Because of this, resolutions are disposable.

They are recomputed whenever context changes.

```
Configuration

↓

Resolve

↓

Offer Resolution
```

The configuration survives.

The resolution evolves.



 Historical Truth Is Permanent

An Order Snapshot is fundamentally different.

Once payment has been accepted, the transaction becomes history.

History must never be recalculated.

```
Offer Resolution

↓

Checkout

↓

Order Snapshot
```

Order Snapshots are immutable legal records.

Future rule changes must never affect historical transactions.



 Deterministic by Design

Determinism is one of Cartique's strongest guarantees.

Every calculation is explainable.

Every result is reproducible.

Every output has a lineage.

Instead of asking:

> "Why did the system charge R1,275?"

The platform should be able to answer:

```
Base Price

↓

Bulk Discount

↓

VIP Discount

↓

Regional Tax

↓

Final Price
```

Commercial truth must always be explainable.



 Extensible Without Modification

New commerce paradigms should not require changes to the kernel.

Instead they extend it.

Examples include:

 Rentals
 Financing
 Loyalty
 Reservations
 Auctions
 Gift Cards
 Digital Licensing
 Marketplace Integrations

These are providers.

Not architectural changes.

The kernel remains stable while capabilities evolve around it.



 Operations Follow Truth

The compiler computes.

The workflow acts.

These responsibilities never overlap.

```
Configuration

↓

Resolution

↓

Commerce Event

↓

Workflow

↓

Side Effects
```

Only workflows may:

 reserve inventory
 capture payments
 print shipping labels
 notify customers
 update accounting systems

The compiler remains mathematically pure.



 The Long-Term Vision

Cartique is intentionally small.

Its goal is not to contain every commerce feature.

Its goal is to provide a stable commercial foundation upon which any commerce model can be built.

Every design decision follows one simple principle:

> Prefer timeless concepts over temporary features.

Features evolve.

Markets change.

Technology changes.

Commerce changes.

Well-defined concepts endure.

That belief forms the philosophical foundation of Cartique.



 Guiding Principle

Cartique does not attempt to model every business.

It models the universal language of commerce.

Everything else is simply composition.
